# Admin Feature Workflow (Categories Reference)

> **Companion rule** to `Crave-bakery-project-brief.mdc`. The brief defines *what* to build; this rule defines *how* to structure each admin feature. Use **Categories** as the canonical reference.

---

## 1. Layer Responsibilities

| Layer | Responsibility | Categories example |
|---|---|---|
| **Controller** | Thin orchestration only: read request → call service/scopes → return `Inertia::render()` or `redirect()->route()` | `CategoryController` |
| **Service** | Multi-step logic, transactions, tree building, aggregations, domain rules | `CategoryService::tree()`, `reorder()`, `stats()`, `wouldCreateCycle()` |
| **Model scopes** | Reusable single-query filters (`blank()` guard, chainable) | `scopeSearch`, `scopeStatus`, `scopeOrdered` |
| **FormRequest** | All validation + `authorize()` via Policy; use `$request->validated()` only | `StoreCategoryRequest`, `UpdateCategoryRequest`, `ReorderCategoryRequest` |
| **Validation trait** | Shared rules between Store/Update when fields overlap | `ValidatesCategory` |
| **Resource** | Shape all data sent to Inertia — never pass raw models | `CategoryResource`, `CategoryTreeResource` |
| **Policy** | Model-level auth via `User::hasPermission('{scope}', '{action}')` | `CategoryPolicy` |
| **Vue page** | Page shell + layout; extract reusable UI to `Components/Admin/` | `Pages/Admin/Categories/Index.vue` + `CategoryTreeList.vue` |

### Scope vs Service — when to use which

- **Use a model scope** when filtering/sorting a flat Eloquent query (`search`, `status`, `ordered`).
- **Use a service** when logic spans multiple records, needs transactions, builds nested structures, or encodes business rules (tree filter keeping ancestors, drag-and-drop reorder, cycle detection, KPI stats).
- **Controller** may chain scopes directly for simple list views; delegate to service for anything non-trivial.

